{"name":"Pax","hex":"#c8c6da","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Pax","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The goddess and personification of peace; one of the Horae, she is a daughter of Jupiter and Themis, and her sisters are Justitia (Justice) and Disciplina / Eunomia (Order of Law); her Greek counterpart is Eirene.","A female given name from Latin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Pax"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peace","blocks":[{"text":"Peace is a state of harmony in the absence of hostility and violence. In a societal sense, peace is commonly used to mean a lack of conflict and freedom from fear of violence between individuals or groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Peace_dove_%283329620077%29.jpg/330px-Peace_dove_%283329620077%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace","wikidata":"Q454"},{"title":"Pax (goddess)","blocks":[{"text":"Pax, more commonly known in English as Peace, was the Roman goddess of peace derived and adopted from the ancient Greek equivalent Eirene. Pax was seen as the daughter of the Roman king god Jupiter and the goddess Justice. Worship of Peace was organized and made popular during the rule of the emperor Augustus who used her imagery to help stabilise the empire after the years of turmoil and civil war of the late republic. Augustus commissioned an altar of peace in her honour on the Campus Martius called Ara Pacis, and the emperor Vespasian built a temple for her on called the Templum Pacis. Pax had a festival held for her on January 30. In art she is commonly depicted holding out olive branches as a peace offering, as well as a caduceus, cornucopia, corn and a sceptre. Pax is also often associated with spring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Statue_of_the_goddess_Pax_%28Pavlovsk_Garden%2C_St_Petersburg%2C_Russia%29.jpg/330px-Statue_of_the_goddess_Pax_%28Pavlovsk_Garden%2C_St_Petersburg%2C_Russia%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pax_(goddess)","wikidata":"Q1132674"},{"title":"PAX (event)","blocks":[{"text":"PAX is a series of gaming culture festivals involving tabletop, arcade, and video gaming. PAX is held annually in Seattle, Boston, Philadelphia, and Melbourne. Previously, it was also held in San Antonio."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/PAX_new_logo%2C_blue.svg/330px-PAX_new_logo%2C_blue.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PAX_(event)","wikidata":"Q588487"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG/330px-Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG/330px-Eir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of Eirene, goddess of peace in ancient Greek religion, with the infant Plutus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEir%C3%A9n%C3%A9_et_Ploutos.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg/250px-Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg/250px-Beowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Before the word 'peace' came into English lexicon, Anglo-Saxons used a phrase \"friðu sibb\" for \"pledge of peace\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_frithu_sibb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Kroisos_BMC_31.jpg/330px-Kroisos_BMC_31.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Kroisos_BMC_31.jpg/330px-Kroisos_BMC_31.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Croeseid coin of Croesus (c. 550 BCE), depicting the Lion and Bull — partly symbolizing alliance between Lydia and Greece, respectively","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKroisos_BMC_31.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Jean_Henri_Dunant.jpg/500px-Jean_Henri_Dunant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Jean_Henri_Dunant.jpg/1280px-Jean_Henri_Dunant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Jean_Henri_Dunant.jpg/500px-Jean_Henri_Dunant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Henry Dunant was awarded the first Nobel Peace Prize for his role in founding the International Red Cross.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJean_Henri_Dunant.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Mohandas_K._Gandhi%2C_portrait.jpg/500px-Mohandas_K._Gandhi%2C_portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Mohandas_K._Gandhi%2C_portrait.jpg/1280px-Mohandas_K._Gandhi%2C_portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Mohandas_K._Gandhi%2C_portrait.jpg/500px-Mohandas_K._Gandhi%2C_portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm